1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,625
Hi — I'm Divij Pawar. This is part one of a two-part

2
00:00:02,667 --> 00:00:04,500
series on cross-agent validation.

3
00:00:05,208 --> 00:00:07,333
Two AI agents look at the same company.

4
00:00:07,708 --> 00:00:10,792
One says revenue grew twelve percent. The other says eight. Which is

5
00:00:10,792 --> 00:00:13,500
right? You don't know. I don't know. Neither does the system that

6
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:15,667
produced them. But you do know one of them is wrong.

7
00:00:16,410 --> 00:00:19,618
Here's the whole idea in one breath. Checking whether an AI's answer

8
00:00:19,618 --> 00:00:22,118
is correct needs ground truth you usually don't have.

9
00:00:22,577 --> 00:00:25,035
Checking whether two AIs disagree needs nothing at all.

10
00:00:25,410 --> 00:00:28,160
So you stop building a truth detector and build a problem detector

11
00:00:28,160 --> 00:00:28,960
instead.

12
00:00:29,452 --> 00:00:32,035
This half of the video is what the research says that actually

13
00:00:32,035 --> 00:00:35,160
takes — and the traps that make naive versions worse than doing

14
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:35,960
nothing.

15
00:00:36,190 --> 00:00:38,398
Start with the thing nobody wants to say out loud.

16
00:00:38,815 --> 00:00:42,273
If an agent hands you a paragraph of confident financial analysis, you

17
00:00:42,273 --> 00:00:43,648
have almost no way to check it.

18
00:00:44,023 --> 00:00:46,607
You could read its reasoning — but the reasoning is also generated

19
00:00:46,607 --> 00:00:47,407
text.

20
00:00:47,315 --> 00:00:50,648
A model that confabulated the conclusion will happily confabulate a convincing

21
00:00:50,648 --> 00:00:54,023
explanation for it. That's not a rare failure. It's the default:

22
00:00:54,398 --> 00:00:57,648
any system graded only by its own output will always look consistent,

23
00:00:58,190 --> 00:01:00,857
because consistency is all it was ever asked to produce.

24
00:01:01,273 --> 00:01:03,857
The system runs. That was never proof that it's right.

25
00:01:04,670 --> 00:01:07,128
The cheapest version of this is to ask the same model the

26
00:01:07,128 --> 00:01:09,503
same question twice, and see if the answers match.

27
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:11,212
That's self-consistency sampling.

28
00:01:11,795 --> 00:01:15,128
Its own documentation is blunt: two identical confabulations are still

29
00:01:15,128 --> 00:01:18,295
confabulations. High consistency is weak positive evidence.

30
00:01:18,712 --> 00:01:22,462
Low consistency is strong negative evidence. Read that asymmetry again — it's

31
00:01:22,462 --> 00:01:26,045
what makes the whole field work. Agreement proves almost nothing. Disagreement

32
00:01:26,045 --> 00:01:27,045
proves something real,

33
00:01:27,628 --> 00:01:29,128
with no ground truth required.

34
00:01:29,545 --> 00:01:33,003
But two samples from one model aren't two opinions. They're two draws

35
00:01:33,003 --> 00:01:34,128
from one distribution.

36
00:01:34,503 --> 00:01:36,045
The fix is to change who answers.

37
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:40,192
Two different agents, compare, flag the mismatch — done? Not quite. Trap

38
00:01:40,192 --> 00:01:41,525
one: information asymmetry.

39
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:44,150
Debate seeks truth when agents have seen different evidence.

40
00:01:44,567 --> 00:01:47,275
When they've seen the same evidence, it's a persuasion contest — one

41
00:01:47,275 --> 00:01:48,817
witness asked twice in two accents.

42
00:01:49,275 --> 00:01:52,233
Trap two is worse, because the obvious fix causes it. They disagree,

43
00:01:52,233 --> 00:01:54,400
so you average, or add a third agent and vote.

44
00:01:54,858 --> 00:01:58,650
But averaging and majority voting amplify shared errors instead of cancelling them.

45
00:01:59,317 --> 00:02:02,317
Ensembling only cancels error when errors are independent.

46
00:02:02,817 --> 00:02:05,567
A majority of correlated agents is not a majority.

47
00:02:06,470 --> 00:02:09,720
Once you're detecting disagreement instead of resolving it, most disagreements turn

48
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:10,520
out to be boring.

49
00:02:10,887 --> 00:02:12,428
The literature sorts them four ways.

50
00:02:12,845 --> 00:02:16,095
Stylistic — same substance, different words. Noise; flag it and you'll cry

51
00:02:16,095 --> 00:02:19,262
wolf. Reasoning — different paths, different conclusions. That's the real thing.

52
00:02:19,720 --> 00:02:22,178
High-confidence disagreement — both sure, mutually exclusive.

53
00:02:22,553 --> 00:02:26,553
Surface it; don't synthesize a false consensus. And adversarial — agents faking

54
00:02:26,553 --> 00:02:27,470
agreement. Then,

55
00:02:27,887 --> 00:02:30,720
staged detection: a cheap classifier handles about

56
00:02:31,095 --> 00:02:34,762
seventy-three percent of pairs with no model call. Only the uncertain ones

57
00:02:34,762 --> 00:02:35,562
escalate.

58
00:02:35,762 --> 00:02:40,095
Sixty-two percent lower cost, ninety point eight percent accuracy held. A check

59
00:02:40,095 --> 00:02:42,762
you can afford to run on everything beats a better check you

60
00:02:42,762 --> 00:02:43,562
run on a sample.

61
00:02:44,190 --> 00:02:46,607
So what does the mature version of this look like? The most

62
00:02:46,607 --> 00:02:50,690
complete systems in the research run many specialized agents across multiple phases,

63
00:02:50,982 --> 00:02:52,607
evaluated on hundreds of questions.

64
00:02:53,190 --> 00:02:55,773
One published ablation study tells you what actually matters.

65
00:02:56,190 --> 00:02:59,732
Remove the adversarial debate, and faithfulness drops zero point one one nine

66
00:02:59,732 --> 00:03:01,232
— the largest drop by far.

67
00:03:01,607 --> 00:03:03,690
Remove the elegant source-weighting matrix, and it costs

68
00:03:04,065 --> 00:03:06,315
zero point zero zero six. Nothing.

69
00:03:06,940 --> 00:03:09,690
The sophisticated machinery is the least important part.

70
00:03:10,065 --> 00:03:13,648
What matters is having something whose job is to disagree. That system

71
00:03:13,648 --> 00:03:17,607
then resolves the disagreement. But arbitration needs heterogeneity you can verify.

72
00:03:18,023 --> 00:03:21,273
If you can't prove your agents fail independently, arbitration hides your only

73
00:03:21,273 --> 00:03:23,857
evidence that something's wrong. So surface it instead.

74
00:03:24,232 --> 00:03:27,523
Machines verify conformance. Humans verify adequacy.

75
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:32,535
So, part one in four lines. Correctness needs ground truth. Disagreement doesn't.

76
00:03:32,702 --> 00:03:36,410
Different evidence matters more than a different model. Never resolve what you

77
00:03:36,410 --> 00:03:37,785
can't prove is independent.

78
00:03:38,327 --> 00:03:41,035
And a cheap check that runs on everything beats a better one

79
00:03:41,035 --> 00:03:42,243
that runs on a sample.

80
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:46,170
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: "Take two AI agents I already

81
00:03:46,170 --> 00:03:46,970
run.

82
00:03:46,795 --> 00:03:49,378
What different evidence would each need to see for their disagreement to

83
00:03:49,378 --> 00:03:51,753
mean something about the world, instead of something about my prompt?"

84
00:03:52,253 --> 00:03:53,053
That's the

85
00:03:53,003 --> 00:03:55,795
information-asymmetry trap, aimed at your own stack. Look for whether Claude can

86
00:03:55,795 --> 00:03:58,420
name two genuinely different sources — if it can't, you don't have

87
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:00,837
two witnesses, you have one witness asked twice.

88
00:04:01,337 --> 00:04:02,295
Part two: the code.

89
00:04:02,930 --> 00:04:07,263
Correctness is not mechanically decidable. Disagreement is. Part two — what I

90
00:04:07,263 --> 00:04:08,063
actually built. Divij Pawar.
